<section><h2>Figure 2. Similar reversal trends, distinct challenge responses</h2>
<p>Wave-pinning and Goryachev both show increasing fixed-support scalar-strength duration over observed successes, but their magnitude, eligibility and censoring differ. This is qualitative similarity, not a shared scaling law.</p>
<p>Incumbent material increases in every model, but concentration, width, initial responsiveness and local restoration change differently.</p>
<p>Substrate availability and a declared local deficit assay jointly support a coupled activation/restoration explanation in Goryachev; their correlation does not partition reversal time.</p>
<p>A strong challenger is insufficient: 23 of 89 activated primary exposures retain both original and challenger cores. Corrected deBelly39 exposes additional incumbent return after a shorter exposure.</p>
<p>Transport and mechanical interventions show causal contributions under specified cues. The WP fixed-dose mobility control passes retained orientation while both conditions fail A&#x27;s stronger strength gate; fourfold inactive diffusion acts throughout cue and recovery. Cue geometry changes intervention effects, so no single activity signature uniquely identifies an explanation.</p>
<a href="../../figure2/figure2_mechanisms.png"><img src="../../figure2/figure2_mechanisms.png" alt="Figure 2. Similar reversal trends, distinct challenge responses"></a>
</section>
<section><h2>Figure 3. Geometry, cue placement and duration shape retained polarity</h2>
<p>Equal physical separation gives a smaller outward-normal angular difference on a larger ring.</p>
<p>At fixed Holmes circumference, changing challenger position alters the finite spatial endpoint from one spanning region to distinct old- and cue-associated peaks.</p>
<p>Strong, opposing local activity can yield a weak global activity vector. The activity angle requires local-pattern and magnitude context.</p>
<p>Finite near cues can leave one full-strength activity region at a graded intermediate angular position; retained angle need not be a binary old/cue outcome.</p>
<p>Distance changes regional depletion, retained angle and vector magnitude differently across native models. The shortest tested successful duration depends on the explicitly chosen endpoint.</p>
<a href="../../figure3/revision_figure3_geometry.png"><img src="../../figure3/revision_figure3_geometry.png" alt="Revised compact geometry sheet; all plotted fields are retained native simulations."></a>
<a href="../../figure3/distance_response/revision_figure3_distance_duration.png"><img src="../../figure3/distance_response/revision_figure3_distance_duration.png" alt="Compact four-model duration-response continuation with native Spring profile evidence."></a>
</section>
<section><h2>Figure 4 · Directional memory and physical site dynamics</h2>
<p>Angular-memory and physical site-shift measurements can show different size trends. Spring makes the forcing dependence explicit: weak-input lag-800 direction cosine rises .584/.832/.958 across sizes despite nonmonotone basal-arc RMS, whereas stronger input gives .019/−.017/.028 with substantial mean activity remaining and late folded shifts near the uniform-angle reference. All three main models retain their native units and controls.</p>
<p>Pairwise coordinate remapping exposes the geometric part of site-angle retention, while local-region and magnitude readouts distinguish observable organizations without classifying abrupt events or inferring competition.</p>
